{%- if kind == "loop" -%}
A loop needed more iterations than the bound unrolls. Constrain what determines the trip count, summarize the loop if it
is not what your property is about, or skip the property and say what bound it needed.
{%- elif kind == "overflow" -%}
`Cannot overflow` is the sound-math pass failing to show that an expression it takes for a pointer stays below 2^64.
Bound the account-data values on that rule's path once — `cvlr_assume!(x < 1 << 63)` — and record each bound as an
assumption. If the rule stops on it again with the bound in place, the bound is not what it needs, and no rewording of
the rule reliably gets past it: which expressions the prover takes for pointers depends on how the code is laid out, not
on what it means. Then state the property against the program's own logic below the handler, a function that
takes no AccountInfo, if the program has one; otherwise skip the property and give the stop as the reason. A property
about the top of the range cannot take the bound at all: skip it and say so.
{%- else -%}
The prover's own message says what it needed.
{%- endif -%}
